Grey Knight wrote:
> 
> While this probably shouldn't apply to altars, does anyone else think
> it would be interesting to have a player-to-player trade/gift system on
> NAO? I've been considering writing a patch to do that, but I'd like to
> know if there's any interest in the idea first. I would probably choose
> to do gifts by having the mail daemon deliver a package ("box of
> holding" to stop players becoming encumbered at inconvenient moments?)
> which contains the object and optionally a message.

I find the idea very appealing, mostly _technically_ but also just for
the fun that you can get presents or help in difficult situations. 8-)
Especially the mail daemon delivery is technically a very nice idea.

But.. It would take out much of the challenge of the game. Part of the
demanding gameplay of Nethack is that there is not always available
what would be nice or even necessary to have, you have to solve puzzles.

While introducing a very nice new game mechanism this patch would make
some of the essentials of Nethack void. IMO.

Such a feature would mostly (only?) be effective on public servers; but
on public servers there is some competition that you would unbalance
with such a patch.

I think it would make Nethack a new type of game, an easier one. I think
it would become an interesting variant if installed on an own dedicated
server.

> Trading would be a bit harder... to guard against cheating,

Well, tha whole patch would be kind of a cheating patch. :-)

> we need the mail daemon to act as an arbitrater:
> [...trading procedure...]

I'll restrict the patch to one-way trading (and a code of trust/ethics)
to keep it technically simple.

> Obviously unique objects cannot be sent to other players. Artifacts
> will resist the attempt, unique corpses turn into slime molds or
> somesuch?

But why? If the artifacts are not yet existing in the game you could
pass them to the other game. And if they are already existing you can
either block the transfer or (as already implemented with bones files)
change them to the plain non-artifact base object.

